  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nIn the wake of a departing shortwave, additional showers and\nthunderstorms are expected to develop across Indiana and Ohio\nbefore advancing east into western Pennsylvania within west-to-\neast flow. Above-normal precipitable water values of 1.7 to 1.9\ninches, combined with increasing instability as skies partially\nclear over Ohio, will support efficient rainfall production with\nrates of 2 to 5 inches per hour. These intense rainfall rates\nwill likely lead to flash flooding, particular long small \ncreeks and streams, in poor drainage locations, and across urban\nareas. \n\nHigh-resolution guidance, including the HREF and REFS, has\nconsistently highlighted a 50 to 70 percent probability of\nrainfall exceeding flash flood guidance in 3 hrs. Localized \nswaths of heavy rainfall could produce rapid rises along creeks \nand streams, causing water to spill over banks, inundate low-\nlying roadways, overwhelm drainage systems, and threaten \nresidual and commercial properties. \n\nThe secondary threat this afternoon would be isolated downburst\nwinds. Mid-level dry air entrainment may enhance downdraft\nstrength, allowing localized straigh-line wind gusts to reach\nthe surface. Icy cores would need to exceed 25kft to likely\nbecome considered severe. \n\nThe probability of heavy rain associated with thunderstorms\ndecreases between 8pm and 11pm tonight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA cold front is expected to track through the region over the\nnext 24 hours from the north. Remnant moisture ahead of the\napproaching cold front will support the development of isolated\nshowers and thunderstorms south of Pittsburgh between 12pm and\n3pm on Saturday. With flow moving parallel to the cold front\n(westerly), there could be some training showers and\nthunderstorms that may prompt localized flooding south of I-70. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \nModel ensembles indicate a ridge should build eastward from the\nCentral CONUS through at least the middle of next week. This \nshould result in dry and gradually warmer weather through much \nof the period with rising 500 mb heights. Highs could again \nreach the lower 90s by Tuesday and Wednesday next week.\n\nThe probability of reaching major heat impacts (where it \naffects anyone without cooling/hydration as well as health \nsystems and industries) ranges between 40 to 55% across the \nregion Wednesday into Friday . The elevated risk is driven by\nmultiple consecutive days with afternoon temperatures near 90F,\ncombined with overnight lows remaining around 70F, limiting\nnighttime cooling and increasing cumulative heat stress.",
